{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794438535,"nonce":"UoiMOAejcIcrq78jTAk6Iy+8Jkw=","title":"An hxcpp program that exits right after a collection can crash on Windows","message":"Found by the first run of tests/counter/run.sh cpp on Windows (CI run 43114004481, hxcpp 4.3.2, MSVC, x64): the program printed PASS and then ended with an access violation, exit status 139 under bash.\n\nWhere, from cdb in CI run 43115426454, which printed every thread's stack at the fault:\n\n- The main thread is in the C runtime's `common_exit`, past the static destructors, called from `Sys.exit`.\n- The faulting thread is one of hxcpp's collector threads: `GlobalAllocator::SThreadLoop` -\u003e `ThreadLoop` -\u003e `finishThreadJob` -\u003e `TAutoLock\u003cHxMutex\u003e` -\u003e `RtlEnterCriticalSection` -\u003e `RtlpWaitOnCriticalSection`.\n- The four `hxn-worker` threads are parked in `hxn_future::pool::park`, and the other collector threads wait on their semaphores. No thread of hxn's is doing anything.\n\nWhy, from hxcpp's src/hx/gc/Immix.cpp: a collection ends by starting a job (`tpjAsyncZero` or `tpjAsyncZeroJit`) that zeroes the reclaimed memory on the collector's threads and is not waited for. Each thread calls `finishThreadJob` when it is done, which locks `sThreadPoolLock`, a static `HxMutex`. On Windows `~HxMutex` is `DeleteCriticalSection`, which `exit` runs with the other threads still going. A thread that finishes its zeroing after that enters a deleted critical section. On other systems the same order destroys a pthread mutex that is then locked, which has not been seen to fail.\n\nSo this is hxcpp's and not hxn's: any hxcpp program on Windows that calls `Sys.exit`, or returns from `main`, within the time the zeroing takes after a collection. The fixture does exactly that: it churns, forces a collection to count what is left, prints and exits. tests/counter/run.sh gen-cpp did not crash in the same run, nor under the debugger; it does more after its collection.\n\nWhat was done: the fixture's two programs end through `Finish.exit` (tests/counter/haxe/Finish.hx), which on hxcpp flushes standard output and calls `_Exit`, so no destructor runs. `Sys.exit` is kept under Emscripten and on HashLink.\n\nOpen:\n- Whether hxn should say or do anything for programs that use it. `hxn.Gc.check` forces a collection when a library's memory is over its budget, and an inbox calls it after each delivery, so a program that uses hxn collects at moments it did not choose, one of which may be just before it exits. hxn has no hook on exit. An `hxn.Loop.exit(code)` that does what `Finish.exit` does would be one answer.\n- Not reported to hxcpp. A fix there would be to stop or wait for the thread jobs before the lock is destroyed, or not to destroy it.\n- The CI step that printed the stacks stays in the workflow: it runs only when a fixture has failed on Windows.","files":null}]}